// Logos standard library — logos.lang.stream
//
// The batch-stream vocabulary of ADR 0025 (`docs/adr/0025-deem-batch-cursor-plane.md`):
// one logical data representation for query execution — a stream of bounded
// batches of typed rows — plus the orthogonal capability traits that record
// what a stream can do.
//
// WHY IT LIVES AT THE LANGUAGE TIER (ADR 0025 §11 [REC]): these are
// general-language notions, the batched sibling of `Iterator`
// (`logos.lang.iter`) the way `ExactSizeIterator` is a sibling in Rust core.
// Rooting them in one supplier (`logos.mem.bt.map`, the query plane, a single
// container family) is the recorded "vocabulary rooted in one supplier"
// defect — every new source would re-declare its own spelling and every
// consumer would branch on which one it holds. The query plane CONSUMES this
// module; it does not own it.
//
// The lang tier is no-alloc/no-OS (`stdlib/lang/logos.module`, tier `lang`),
// so only the protocol, the capabilities and the BORROWED batch view live
// here. `Buffer<R>` — the degenerate one-packet stream over a `Vec` (ADR §4)
// — needs the heap and therefore sits one tier up in `logos.mem.stream`
// (`stdlib/mem/stream/buffer.logos`).
//
// Not in the prelude on purpose: `BatchStream::next` and `Iterator::next` have
// the same name and receiver shape, so putting this module in the implicit
// prelude would make `it.next()` ambiguous for every iterator in the tree.
// Consumers write `use logos.lang.stream;`.

package logos.lang.stream;

use logos.lang.option;

// ── The pull protocol (ADR 0025 §1) ─────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The pull unit is a BATCH, not a row. Row-at-a-time is a CONSUMER pattern
// (the inner loop), not the protocol:
//
//     while let Option::Some(b) = s.next() {
//         let n: i64 = b.len() as i64;
//         let mut i: i64 = 0 as i64;
//         while i < n { /* clauses over b @ i */ i = i + (1 as i64); }
//     }
//
// A heap slice is a stream of EXACTLY ONE batch — the whole `&[T]` — so the
// old indexed loop is literally the inner loop of the degenerate case.

/// A stream of bounded batches of typed rows: the one pull protocol of ADR 0025.
///
/// `next` returns:
/// * `Option::None` — the stream is EXHAUSTED. Terminal: no further call is defined
///   unless the stream is also `Rewind` and has been rewound.
/// * `Option::Some(b)` with `b.len() == 0` — a LEGAL tick, not an end: an empty leaf,
///   an empty epoch (ADR §10), a no-progress/keepalive tick of a budgeted producer.
///   A consumer must treat it as "no rows this time, ask again", never as termination.
///
/// The batch `B` is a typed VIEW over storage (`RowsBatch<R>` row-major, `ColsBatch`
/// columnar), never a copy, and never `dyn` (§7b): the inner loop over a batch is
/// monomorphic and inlined, so no dispatch sits at per-row frequency.
pub trait BatchStream<B> {
    /// Pulls the next batch; `None` = exhausted, `Some(empty)` = a legal no-rows tick.
    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<B>;
}

// ── Capabilities (ADR 0025 §3) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Capabilities are ORTHOGONAL TRAITS on the stream type, not shapes. Existence
// of a capability = trait membership, asked by the planner via metaprog trait
// queries: a declaration can lie about code, a type cannot (the S4c lesson).
// Cost, exactness and the operation set stay DECLARATIONS (`op … exact`,
// `size <rel> = <fn>`, ADR 0024 S6) because cost is data-dependent and
// inexpressible as a type.

/// The stream can be re-landed and replayed from the start: multi-pass.
///
/// Consuming a non-`Rewind` stream twice is a plan-level error whose repair is an
/// inserted `Drain` with its ground (§4). S2j put the DRAIN in place — the planner
/// inserts the node where its single-pass proof used to be recorded
/// (`wql::plan_walker::plan_insert_drains`) — so what this trait still owes is the
/// REFUSAL: today a second read is caught by a planner walking the query, and it
/// should be caught by the type of the thing being read twice.
pub trait Rewind {
    /// Re-lands the stream at the beginning; the next `next()` yields the first batch again.
    fn rewind(&mut self);
}

/// The stream knows its row count for free — without draining it.
///
/// Free means O(1)-ish and side-effect-free: a declared container `size`, a `Vec`
/// length. A source that would have to walk to answer must NOT implement this.
pub trait SizedStream {
    /// Total number of rows the stream will yield, without consuming it.
    fn size(&self) -> u64;
}

/// Marker: batches arrive in `K` order, and each batch is sorted by `K` internally.
///
/// The order fact that today's ten representations record nowhere (family walks and
/// `BTreeMapIter` have it physically; `HashMapIter` does not). Immediate harvest:
/// an `order by` over a column whose source already declares this order is a NO-OP.
/// Carries no method: it is a promise about the `next()` sequence, checked by the
/// producer's own tests, not by a signature.
pub trait OrderedBy<K> {}

/// Ordered positioning: the stream can jump to key `K` (the landing constructors' shape).
///
/// Only meaningful together with `OrderedBy<K>` — seeking a stream with no `K` order
/// is a request nothing can answer. `seek_key` past the last key leaves the stream
/// exhausted (the next `next()` is `None`), never a panic.
///
/// NAMED `seek_key`, NOT `seek` (S1, 2026-08-13). The generated container beneath a
/// family walk already spells the two positionings `seek(n: u64)` = BY ORDINAL and
/// `seek_key(k)` = BY KEY, and a family walk implements `Landed<K>` and
/// `RandomAccess<B>` SIMULTANEOUSLY — so a `Landed::seek(k)` would have put the
/// container's two conventions on one type INVERTED (`s.seek(k)` by key beside
/// `s.seek_nth(n)` by ordinal, over a `c.seek(n)` by ordinal one layer down). The
/// rename was the cheaper of the two repairs the hazard admits — the other being a
/// doc-comment at both sites — and it was cheap precisely because `Landed` had ONE
/// impl (the emitted `{N}LeafWalk`) and one caller when S1 opened. It stops being
/// cheap once S3 consumes the trait.
pub trait Landed<K> {
    /// Positions the stream at the first row with key `>= k`. By KEY — the ordinal
    /// twin is `RandomAccess::seek_nth`.
    fn seek_key(&mut self, k: K);
}

// ── The TRAVERSAL axis (ADR 0025 §3, Victor 2026-08-11) ─────────────────────
//
// A THIRD orthogonal capability axis beside order-as-fact (`OrderedBy<K>`) and
// key-positioning (`Landed<K>`):
//
//     forward-only ⊂ bidirectional ⊂ random-access (by ordinal)
//
// and the ⊂ is the SUPERTRAIT EDGE below, so the lattice lives in the type
// system and cannot drift from the table that describes it. Concretely:
//
//   * forward-only  = a plain `BatchStream` with NEITHER trait. No third name is
//     minted for the bottom of the chain — that is the recorded "`can_seek` as
//     one bool is WRONG" lesson applied in the other direction: the absence of a
//     capability is not a capability. `HashMapIter` sits here (and is not on this
//     plane at all today — it implements `Iterator`, not `BatchStream`).
//   * bidirectional = `Bidirectional<B>`. A btree walk.
//   * random-access = `RandomAccess<B>`, which IMPLIES `Bidirectional<B>`. Family
//     walks, heap slices and `Buffer`.
//
// The S3 harvest this vocabulary is FOR: `offset`/`limit` push down as one
// `seek_nth` instead of a skip loop; `order by … desc` over a bidirectional
// source emits no `Sort`; ordinal sampling needs no drain.

/// Bidirectional traversal: batches can be pulled BACKWARD as well as forward.
///
/// `prev` returns:
/// * `Option::None` — the stream is AT THE START. The exact mirror of `next`'s
///   `None` = exhausted: a terminal answer for this direction, not an error.
/// * `Option::Some(b)` — the batch immediately BEFORE the current position, and the
///   position moves to that batch's first row. An EMPTY batch stays legal in this
///   direction too (§1), for the same reasons.
///
/// Alternating `next`/`prev` therefore hands out the same batch twice, which is the
/// point: `prev` un-consumes, it does not skip.
pub trait Bidirectional<B> {
    /// Pulls the batch before the current position; `None` = already at the start.
    fn prev(&mut self) -> Option<B>;
}

/// Random access BY ENTRY ORDINAL (ADR 0025 §3).
///
/// Every Memoria container is an ARRAY of its entries, so positioning by ordinal is
/// ONE DESCENT — which is exactly what keeps §7b's per-LEAF frequency invariant: no
/// dyn call and no seek is paid per ROW. That is why this is a capability of the
/// stream rather than an adapter that skips.
///
/// POSITIONING IS EXACT AND 0-BASED OVER THE STREAM'S OWN LANDING, not over the
/// container: a narrowed landing's ordinal 0 is ITS first row, not the container's.
/// `seek_nth(n)` with `n` past the landing's last row leaves the stream exhausted
/// (the next `next()` is `None`), never a panic — the same contract `Landed::seek_key`
/// states for a key past the end.
///
/// `size()` (`SizedStream`) is the LANDING's total and is NOT changed by `seek_nth`:
/// seeking moves a POSITION inside a landing, it does not re-land. Re-landing is
/// `Landed::seek_key` (by key) or `Rewind::rewind` (to the start).
pub trait RandomAccess<B>: Bidirectional<B> {
    /// Positions the stream so the next `next()` yields the batch containing row `n`,
    /// counted from the stream's own landing. One descent, never a skip loop.
    fn seek_nth(&mut self, n: u64);
}

// ── The row-major batch (ADR 0025 §2) ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// THE BARE SLICE IS THE BATCH. §2 spells the row-major batch `RowsBatch<R>` =
// `&[R]`, and in Logos that equality can be literal rather than a wrapper:
// `&[R]` already carries `len()` and `[i]`, which is the whole batch surface
// the emitted scan loop uses. A struct wrapper would add a field to unwrap at
// every access, a lifetime knot to re-tie, and a second spelling of `len` —
// paying for nothing. So `RowsBatch<R>` is an ALIAS, and
// `impl BatchStream<&[R]> for …` / `impl BatchStream<RowsBatch<R>> for …` are
// the same impl (both spellings measured compiling, 2026-08-11).
//
// The alias still earns its place: it is the name the emitter and the ADR use,
// and it is where a future selection-vector or bounds-carrying row batch would
// land WITHOUT touching the impls that spell it by name (selection vectors are
// deliberately out of v1 — recorded as an axis).

/// The row-major batch of ADR 0025 §2: a borrowed window of `R` rows.
///
/// Literally `&[R]` — the slice IS the batch (`len()` + `[i]` is the whole surface
/// the scan loop needs), so heap collections, fixpoint outputs and deltas hand out
/// their storage directly and the batch costs nothing to build. The columnar
/// counterpart (`ColsBatch`/`PdtCol` over a Memoria leaf's PdtBuf slots) is a
/// separate type, selected by the source's declaration, not by a branch.
pub type RowsBatch<R> = &[R];
